---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import {latestVersion} from "../lib/docs";
import {repoUrl} from "../lib/navigation";
import {generatedPage} from "../lib/pages";

const {description, sourcePath, title} = generatedPage("/download");
const version = latestVersion();
const releaseUrl = `${repoUrl}/releases/tag/${version}`;
const image = `ghcr.io/radiergummi/tsrelay:${version.slice(1)}`;

/**
 * One entry per binary the release workflow builds: its goos × goarch matrix,
 * named `tsrelay_<os>_<arch>`, with `.exe` on Windows.
 */
const platforms = [
    {name: "macOS", builds: [{arch: "arm64", label: "Apple silicon"}, {arch: "amd64", label: "Intel"}], os: "darwin"},
    {name: "Linux", builds: [{arch: "amd64", label: "x86-64"}, {arch: "arm64", label: "ARM64"}], os: "linux"},
    {name: "Windows", builds: [{arch: "amd64", label: "x86-64"}, {arch: "arm64", label: "ARM64"}], os: "windows"},
].map(({builds, name, os}) => ({
    name,
    builds: builds.map(({arch, label}) => {
        const file = `tsrelay_${os}_${arch}${os === "windows" ? ".exe" : ""}`;

        return {
            label,
            file,
            url: `${repoUrl}/releases/download/${version}/${file}`,
            checksumUrl: `${repoUrl}/releases/download/${version}/tsrelay_${os}_${arch}.sha256`,
        };
    }),
}));

const headings = [
    {depth: 2, slug: "binaries", text: "Binaries"},
    {depth: 2, slug: "container-image", text: "Container image"},
    {depth: 2, slug: "verifying-a-download", text: "Verifying a download"},
];
---
<DocsLayout title={title} description={description} slug="download" headings={headings} sourcePath={sourcePath}>
    <p>
        The latest release is <a href={releaseUrl}>{version}</a>. One binary is both the relay
        (<code>tsrelay serve</code>) and the CLI developers use to receive callbacks.
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout-tip">
        <div class="callout-header">Using a relay someone else runs?</div>
        <p>
            Install the CLI from your relay instead, as <a href="/getting-started">Getting started</a> describes:
            you get the version your relay runs, and <code>tsrelay update</code> keeps the two
            in step.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="binaries">Binaries</h2>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Platform</th><th>Binary</th><th>Checksum</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {platforms.flatMap(({name, builds}) => builds.map(({checksumUrl, file, label, url}) => (
                <tr>
                    <td>{name}, {label}</td>
                    <td><a href={url} download>{file}</a></td>
                    <td><a href={checksumUrl}>SHA-256</a></td>
                </tr>
            )))}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>On macOS and Linux, put the binary on your <code>PATH</code> and make it executable:</p>

    <pre><code>{`curl -fsSLo tsrelay ${platforms[1].builds[0].url}
chmod +x tsrelay
sudo mv tsrelay /usr/local/bin/`}</code></pre>

    <p>
        The macOS binaries are signed with a Developer ID and notarized. Every release, with its notes and
        older versions, is on <a href={`${repoUrl}/releases`}>GitHub</a>. To run the relay, continue
        with <a href="/running-a-relay">Running a relay</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="container-image">Container image</h2>

    <p>
        The relay is also published as a container image for <code>linux/amd64</code> and
        <code>linux/arm64</code>. Its tags follow the release: <code>{version.slice(1)}</code>, the
        minor and major versions, and <code>latest</code>.
    </p>

    <pre><code>{`docker pull ${image}`}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Ready-made deployments are in the repository, for <a href={`${repoUrl}/tree/main/deploy/kubernetes`}>Kubernetes</a> and <a href={`${repoUrl}/blob/main/deploy/compose.yaml`}>Docker Compose</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="verifying-a-download">Verifying a download</h2>

    <p>
        Each release binary comes with a SHA-256 checksum, a signed build provenance attestation, and a signed
        CycloneDX SBOM listing the modules it was built from. With the GitHub CLI:
    </p>

    <pre><code>{`gh attestation verify tsrelay_linux_amd64 -R Radiergummi/tsrelay                              # provenance
gh attestation verify tsrelay_linux_amd64 -R Radiergummi/tsrelay --predicate-type https://cyclonedx.org/bom`}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Without access to GitHub's API, verify against the bundles attached to the release: add
        <code>--bundle tsrelay.provenance.sigstore.json</code> or
        <code>--bundle tsrelay_linux_amd64.sbom.sigstore.json</code>. The SBOM itself is
        <code>tsrelay_linux_amd64.cdx.json</code>.
    </p>
</DocsLayout>
